  "query_preview": "..\n(Saturday through next Thursday)\nIssued at 1205 PM CDT Thu Sep 3 2026\n\nDespite the dissolving of Edouard's moisture and shear aloft by\nSaturday, the trailing TUTT Low is moving onshore into Deep South\nTX and the Coastal Bend by this time to bring one more day of\nslightly elevated rain chances. Pooled moisture in the mid levels\n(PWat values near 2.2 inches) and some more weak mid-upper level \nshear might be just enough to bring one more day of low CHC\ncategory PoPs at least in the daytime. This also should curb high\ntemperatures, but probably not as much as we expect it to on\nFriday. Sunday's rain prospects look even more isolated, because\nby then, the TUTT circulation will have passed to the SW into\nMexico, leaving only the moist easterly mid level winds. High\ntemperatures will continue trending back toward triple digits, but\nour blended guidances continue to show the I-35 corridor remaining\nbelow triple digits. By Monday, the upper ridging should further\nisolate rain chances, but not completely eliminate them. The ridge\ncenter over Arkansas means a deep onshore wind extends up to the\nH5 level and that pattern is expected to hold up through the\nmiddle of next week. So with compliments from Edouard, the season\nof triple digit heat wave have come to an end for all but the Rio\nGrande Plains. The elevated heat indices are not expected to go\naway as quietly, and some 100-105 values each day will be in\nconsideration throughout the next 7 days.",
